Step 0 already exposes the key distinction: the paper’s general feasibility algorithm is exponential (configuration-graph cycle detection), while the unweighted special case is exactly edge coloring; the latter has a polynomial Δ+1-color algorithm but deciding whether a cubic graph needs 3 or 4 colors is NP-complete. I’m now reading the constructions and checking whether an inverse-generated, genuinely native scheduling family can avoid collapsing into an easy planted-coloring distribution.
