+3-edge-colourability of cubic graphs, but that worst-case theorem does not make
+an inverse-planted distribution hard. Indeed, random planted cubic graphs were
+rejected during construction because randomized DPLL found their colourings.
 
-The chosen object comes from Section 5, Proposition 5.1.  In an unweighted OPS
-instance a heat-d schedule exists exactly when the graph is d-edge-colourable.
-By Lemma 2.1, the same period-d colouring is a DPS schedule when every
-frequency is d.  The
-paper's hardness theorem comes from 3-regular chromatic index, but it is only a
-worst-case statement and says nothing about this generated distribution.
-Bipartite edge colouring is polynomial, and this affine subfamily is easier
