-Certificate production.  In every disjoint panel choose a prime q, a nonzero
-slope a, and d distinct offsets B before emitting any edge.  For every x in
-Z/qZ emit the d edges L_x--R_(a*x+b), b in B.  For a fixed b this is a perfect
-matching because a is nonzero; the d matchings partition all edges.  Combining
-the same-numbered matching from each disjoint panel gives a period-d schedule
-meeting every frequency-d deadline.  Generation therefore knows the symbolic
-schedule by inverse generation.  The verifier independently expands every
-rule, checks exact edge coverage and matching incidence, and checks the
-recurrence bound.  It never reads answer.
+Generation. Sample nonzero slope and step and a start first. Multiply
+Q(z)=product_j(z-start-j*step), then substitute z=y-slope*x. The answer therefore
+exists by composition of polynomial identities. Each factor is a perfect
+matching between two copies of F_p, and distinct progression terms partition
+all relationships into n matchings. Three seed-dependent disjoint cycles alter
+the graph isomorphism type without marking any core edge; they have a fixed
+canonical schedule within the same n days.
 
-Easy and mechanical routes.  Misra--Gries gives a polynomial (Delta+1)-edge
-colouring for general graphs, and regular bipartite graphs admit exact Delta
-edge colourings by repeated perfect matching extraction.  For the certificate
-language used here, a direct exhaustive algorithm derives the offset set from
-one row for each of q-1 slopes and compares rows until one works.  Its measured
-cost is the Track-B reference baseline.  The compact route instead observes
-sum N(x)=d*a*x+sum(B) mod q; two displayed rows determine a, after which one
-row determines B.
-
-Adversaries.  Slopes 1 and the first small slopes are excluded from the planted
-distribution; endpoint order is independently shuffled; the first edges of
-the first two rows use different offsets; and the first two row minima are
-chosen not to align under the true translation.  Thus identity residues,
-single-edge extrapolation, a greedy minimum-neighbour translation, a small
-slope scan, and uniform bounded random restarts all fail in the audited panel.
-All graph edges still come from one distribution--there are no planted-edge
-and decoy-edge populations.
-
-Canonicalization.  Coordinates are part of the symbolic scheduling instance,
-while row order, neighbour order, affine changes of the left and right residue
-coordinates, swapping the bipartition, permuting panels, and permuting days are presentation
-symmetries.  The key recovers the offset set from graph data (not answer), then
-normalizes it under every affine map b -> u*b+t.  G8 composes all of the named
-symmetries and carries the witness through them.
+Easy results avoided. Misra-Gries gives Delta+1 colours in polynomial time, and
+Theorems 1.5 and 1.6 give polynomial-time approximation schedules. Those are
+not hidden: they are insufficient for the requested optimal n-day symbolic
+schedule, while the exact root scan is reported as the successful Track B
+reference algorithm. Cheap coefficient guesses, a low-degree greedy guess,
+uniform restarts, and the by-hand unit-step ansatz are measured and fail.
